    13	{"id": "10.1007/s10854-019-02067-5", "title": "Influence of sintering aid on the microstructure and conductivity of the garnet-type W-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 ceramic electrolyte", "authors": ["Yali Luo", "Xueyan Li", "Han Chen", "Lucun Guo"], "year": 2019, "venue": "Journal of Materials Science: Materials in Electronics", "doi": "10.1007/s10854-019-02067-5", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10854-019-02067-5", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 18, "sources": ["crossref"]}
    14	{"id": "W3165675957", "title": "Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte sintered by the ultrafast high-temperature method", "authors": ["Martin Ihrig", "Tarini Prasad Mishra", "Walter Sebastian Scheld", "Grit Häuschen", "Wolfgang Rheinheimer", "Martin Bram", "Martin Finsterbusch", "Olivier Guillon"], "year": 2021, "venue": "Journal of the European Ceramic Society", "doi": "10.1016/j.jeurceramsoc.2021.05.041", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeurceramsoc.2021.05.041", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 82, "sources": ["openalex"]}
    15	{"id": "W3005819298", "title": "Spark plasma sintering plus heat-treatment of Ta-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte and its ionic conductivity", "authors": ["Jiali Xue", "Kuibao Zhang", "Daimeng Chen", "Jianjun Zeng", "Baozhu Luo"], "year": 2020, "venue": "Materials Research Express", "doi": "10.1088/2053-1591/ab7618", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1088/2053-1591/ab7618", "pdf_url": "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/2053-1591/ab7618/pdf", "abstract": "Abstract Ta-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 (Ta-LLZO) solid electrolyte ceramics were synthesized by solid-state sintering, spark plasma sintering (SPS) and SPS plus heat-treatment (two-step method) using LiOH·H2O, La2O3, ZrO2 and Ta2O5 as the raw materials. The Ta-LLZO samples prepared from these three routes are all in cubic phase structure. However, the solid-state method results in Ta-LLZO samples with different particle sizes and porous morphology, while the samples prepared by SPS route contain fewer internal voids and smaller particle size. The two-step method not only retains the high compactness of SPS samples, but also the increment of grain size. The SEM results demonstrate that Ta-LLZO sintered by the two-step method shows optimal electrical properties. And the total ionic conductivity is 4.6 × 10−3 S cm−1 at 150 °C and the activation energy is calculated to be 0.38 eV.", "citation_count": 34, "sources": ["openalex"]}
    16	{"id": "10.1016/j.electacta.2016.11.020", "title": "Sintering Mechanisms of High-Performance Garnet-type Solid Electrolyte Densified by Spark Plasma Sintering", "authors": ["Hirotoshi Yamada", "Tomoko Ito", "Rajendra Hongahally Basappa"], "year": 2016, "venue": "Electrochimica Acta", "doi": "10.1016/j.electacta.2016.11.020", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.electacta.2016.11.020", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 85, "sources": ["crossref"]}
    17	{"id": "W2281878692", "title": "Ionic Conductivity and Air Stability of Al-Doped Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 Sintered in Alumina and Pt Crucibles", "authors": ["Wenhao Xia", "Biyi Xu", "Huanan Duan", "Yiping Guo", "Hongmei Kang", "Hua Li", "Hezhou Liu"], "year": 2016, "venue": "ACS Applied Materials & Interfaces", "doi": "10.1021/acsami.5b12186", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1021/acsami.5b12186", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": "Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is a promising electrolyte material for all-solid-state battery due to its high ionic conductivity and good stability with metallic lithium. In this article, we studied the effect of crucibles on the ionic conductivity and air stability by synthesizing 0.25Al doped LLZO pellets in Pt crucibles and alumina crucibles, respectively. The results show that the composition and microstructure of the pellets play important roles influencing the ionic conductivity, relative density, and air stability. Specifically, the 0.25Al-LLZO pellets sintered in Pt crucibles exhibit a high relative density (∼96%) and high ionic conductivity (4.48 × 10(-4) S cm(-1)). The ionic conductivity maintains 3.6 × 10(-4) S cm(-1) after 3-month air exposure. In contrast, the ionic conductivity of the pellets from alumina crucibles is about 1.81 × 10(-4) S cm(-1) and drops to 2.39 × 10(-5) S cm(-1) 3 months later. The large grains and the reduced grain boundaries in the pellets sintered in Pt crucibles are favorable to obtain high ionic conductivity and good air stability. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and Raman spectroscopy results suggest that the formation of Li2CO3 on the pellet surface is probably another main reason, which is also closely related to the relative density and the amount of grain boundary within the pellets. This work stresses the importance of synthesis parameters, crucibles included, to obtain the LLZO electrolyte with high ionic conductivity and good air stability.", "citation_count": 305, "_query": "LLZO relative density ionic conductivity microstructure", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    18	{"id": "W2913888676", "title": "Manipulating Li2O atmosphere for sintering dense Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte", "authors": ["Xiao Huang", "Yang Lu", "Zhen Song", "Kun Rui", "Qingsong Wang", "Tongping Xiu", "Michael E. Badding", "Zhaoyin Wen"], "year": 2019, "venue": "Energy storage materials", "doi": "10.1016/j.ensm.2019.01.018", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ensm.2019.01.018", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 207, "_query": "LLZO relative density ionic conductivity microstructure", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    19	{"id": "W2072635242", "title": "Field assisted sintering of dense Al-substituted cubic phase Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolytes", "authors": ["Yanhua Zhang", "Fei Chen", "Rong Tu", "Qiang Shen", "Lianmeng Zhang"], "year": 2014, "venue": "Journal of Power Sources", "doi": "10.1016/j.jpowsour.2014.03.148", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpowsour.2014.03.148", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 192, "_query": "LLZO relative density ionic conductivity microstructure", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    20	{"id": "W2148558281", "title": "Microstructure and Li‐Ion Conductivity of Hot‐Pressed Cubic Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12", "authors": ["Isabel N. David", "Travis Thompson", "J. Wolfenstine", "Jan L. Allen", "Jeff Sakamoto"], "year": 2015, "venue": "Journal of the American Ceramic Society", "doi": "10.1111/jace.13455", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/jace.13455", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": "The effect of hot‐pressing temperature on the microstructure and Li‐ion transport of Al‐doped, cubic Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 ( LLZO ) was investigated. At fixed pressure (62 MPa), the relative density was 86%, 97%, and 99% when hot‐pressing at 900°C, 1000°C, and 1100°C, respectively. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy showed that the percent grain‐boundary resistance decreased with increasing hot‐pressing temperature. Hot pressing at 1100°C resulted in a total conductivity of 0.37 mS/cm at room temperature where the grain boundaries contributed to 8% of the total resistance; one of the lowest grain‐boundary resistances reported. We believe hot pressing is an appealing technique to minimize grain‐boundary resistance and enable correlations between LLZO composition and bulk ionic conductivity.", "citation_count": 146, "_query": "LLZO relative density ionic conductivity microstructure", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    21	{"id": "2eba9f5eb7fbcd4e011618248394c491540494e3", "title": "A simple and highly efficient method toward high density garnet-type LLZTO solid state electrolyte.", "authors": ["Fei Shen", "Weichang Guo", "Dingyuan Zeng", "Zhouting Sun", "Jie Gao", "Jun Li", "Bin Zhao", "Bo He", "Xiaogang Han"], "year": 2020, "venue": "ACS Applied Materials and Interfaces", "doi": "10.1021/acsami.0c04850", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2eba9f5eb7fbcd4e011618248394c491540494e3", "pdf_url": "", "abstract": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is among the most attractive candidates for achieving solid state lithium batteries. LLZO pellet with high density is preferred due to its potential to prevent dendritic Li growth and penetration. However, the presence of voids inside the LLZO electrolyte is inevitable if prepared by traditional solid state reaction. Large numbers of voids have an adverse influence on both ionic conductivity and density of the LLZO pellets. In this paper, we studied the origin of the voids formation in the Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO) and introduced a fast oxygen assisted sintering method to eliminate the voids. All basic physical properties of the LLZTO sintered in oxygen for only 1 h are better than those in air. The conductivity and Vickers hardness of the LLZTO increase to 6.13×10-4 S cm-1 and 9.84 Gpa, with 12.3% and 62.8% enhancement, respectively, even at a low pre-calcined temperature of 600 oC. Li||Li symmetric cell with the LLZTO sintered in oxygen also shows a more stable and longer cycling at higher current density (0.4 mA cm-2).", "citation_count": 98, "_query": "LLZO LLZTO sintering aid Li3BO3 low temperature sintering", "sources": ["semanticscholar"]}
    22	{"id": "W3012374367", "title": "Enhanced densification and ionic conductivity of Li-garnet electrolyte: Efficient Li2CO3 elimination and fast grain-boundary transport construction", "authors": ["Saisai Zhang", "Hailei Zhao", "Jie Wang", "Tao Xu", "Kangkang Zhang", "Zhihong Du"], "year": 2020, "venue": "Chemical Engineering Journal", "doi": "10.1016/j.cej.2020.124797", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2020.124797", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": "Abstract As promising next generation energy storage system, all solid state lithium batteries offer great potential for achieving high energy density and improved safety over lithium batteries with liquid counterpart. Dense microstructure with high ionic conductivity are prerequisites for solid electrolyte. The promising Li-garnet electrolyte suffers from poor sinterability, which results in porous structure and large grain boundary resistance. Herein, we report that the poor sinterability of garnet Li7La3Zr2O12 is most likely related to the Li2CO3 formed on particle surface, and SiO2 is an efficient additive to scavenge the surface Li2CO3 and so significantly improve the densification of electrolyte. The reaction product Li4SiO4 of SiO2 with Li2CO3 is a Li-ion conductor, which exists at grain boundary and so can facilitate the grain boundary lithium ion diffusion. An addition of 1 mol.% SiO2, with respect to the total lithium content in Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO), significantly increases the relative density of LLZTO pellet by 28% and the conductivity by one order of magnitude, reaching 3.84 × 10−4 S cm−1 at room temperature by a conventional solid state sintering. A symmetric lithium cell assembled with such dense LLZTO electrolyte can cycle well for 260 h compared with unmodified LLZTO running only for 72 h before short-circuit.", "citation_count": 63, "_query": "LLZTO densification grain boundary total conductivity", "sources": ["openalex", "crossref", "semanticscholar"]}
    23	{"id": "W3123760475", "title": "Controlling the lithium proton exchange of LLZO to enable reproducible processing and performance optimization", "authors": ["Melanie Rosen", "Ruijie Ye", "Markus Mann", "Sandra Lobe", "Martin Finsterbusch", "Olivier Guillon", "Dina Fattakhova‐Rohlfing"], "year": 2021, "venue": "Journal of Materials Chemistry A", "doi": "10.1039/d0ta11096e", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1039/d0ta11096e", "pdf_url": "https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlepdf/2021/ta/d0ta11096e", "abstract": "A new, mechanistic understanding of the lithium proton exchange in LLZO enables reproducible processing and performance optimization for tape-cast components.", "citation_count": 63, "_query": "LLZO processing structure property reproducibility batch", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    24	{"id": "08b222686052a076675cb833ba7e26dad754e7db", "title": "Broad temperature dependence, high conductivity, and structure-property relations of cold sintering of LLZO-based composite electrolytes", "authors": ["J. Seo", "Hiroto Nakaya", "Y. Takeuchi", "Z. Fan", "H. Hikosaka", "R. Rajagopalan", "E. Gomez", "M. Iwasaki", "C. Randall"], "year": 2020, "venue": null, "doi": "10.1016/j.jeurceramsoc.2020.06.050", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/08b222686052a076675cb833ba7e26dad754e7db", "pdf_url": "https://scholarsphere.psu.edu/resources/7e3cb16f-6f81-42cf-aa5d-a74e03bb24b9/downloads/20449", "abstract": "Abstract The garnet-type LLZO is one of the most attractive, highly conductive ceramic materials for all-solid-state Li batteries. However, there are so many challenges, such as high temperature sintering for many hours, and air and moisture sensitivity of the materials. In this study, to overcome these obstacles for practical application, we synthesized high conductivity LLZO raw material with Mg, Sr substitution and applied a low temperature sintering process, namely cold sintering, to densify the LLZO-SM-based composites. The LLZO-SM-base composite electrolyte was densified using cold sintering process with DMF as a partially transient liquid phase at 120 °C, and the resulting composite shows a high conductivity of 10−4 S/cm. Moreover, the cold sintered composite electrolyte exhibits high conductivity under a wide range of temperature, as well as good microstructures and grain boundary interfaces. Furthermore, the all-solid-state Li-ion battery with cold sintered composite delivering high capacity was fabricated as a basic demonstration. These promising results demonstrated the feasibility of the cold sintering process for development of solid-state electrolytes and fabrication of all-solid-state Li batteries.", "citation_count": 59, "_query": "LLZO processing structure property reproducibility batch", "sources": ["semanticscholar"]}
    25	{"id": "f3ec71804230952d2804be6e8ac15aecac2e7155", "title": "Effects of CuO on the microstructure and electrochemical properties of garnet-type Li6.3La3Zr1.65W0.35O12 solid electrolyte", "authors": ["Wenqiang Zhang", "Chunwen Sun"], "year": 2019, "venue": null, "doi": "10.1016/j.jpcs.2019.109080", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ec71804230952d2804be6e8ac15aecac2e7155", "pdf_url": "", "abstract": "Abstract The garnet Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 (LLZO)-based oxides have attracted much attention for solid-state lithium (Li)-ion batteries because of high Li ion conductivity and good chemical stability. However, high-temperature sintering at around 1200 °C and a long sintering time above 10 h are normally required to obtain dense LLZO pellets. Dense sintering of solid electrolytes at low temperature or short sintering time is highly desired for developing high-performance solid-state rechargeable batteries. In this work, we investigate the effect of CuO as a sintering aid on the microstructure and electrochemical properties of garnet-type Li 6.3 La 3 Zr 1.65 W 0.35 O 12 (LLZWO) solid electrolyte through conventional solid-state reaction. The addition of a small amount of CuO can effectively lower the sintering temperature of LLZWO to 1120 °C and reduce sintering time to 6 h. The ionic conductivity of the obtained 2 wt% CuO–LLZWO electrolyte is 1.88 × 10 −4  S cm −1  at 25 °C and melted metallic Li shows good wetting behavior toward CuO–LLZWO pellets. Moreover, the LiFePO 4 |2 wt% CuO–LLZWO|Li battery shows a capacity of 144.9 mAh g −1  at 0.1 C and 65 °C. Even at 1 C, the capacity of the cell can remain at 104.7 mAh g −1 . This work offers an effective strategy for preparing high-performance electrolytes for solid-state batteries.", "citation_count": 57, "_query": "LLZO LLZTO sintering aid Li3BO3 low temperature sintering", "sources": ["semanticscholar"]}
    26	{"id": "W2984753931", "title": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 Solid Electrolyte Materials by Sintering in a Lithium-Rich Air Atmosphere", "authors": ["Xingxing Zhang", "Tae-Sik Oh", "Jeffrey W. Fergus"], "year": 2019, "venue": "Journal of The Electrochemical Society", "doi": "10.1149/2.1031915jes", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1149/2.1031915jes", "pdf_url": "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1149/2.1031915jes/pdf", "abstract": "Garnet-type Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li + conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li + conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li 6.75 La 3 Zr 1.75 Ta 0.25 O 12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10 −4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary.", "citation_count": 56, "_query": "LLZTO densification grain boundary total conductivity", "sources": ["openalex", "semanticscholar"]}
    27	{"id": "W4292084314", "title": "Compositional and structural control in LLZO solid electrolytes", "authors": ["Kade Parascos", "Joshua Watts", "José A. Alarco", "Yan Chen", "Peter C. Talbot"], "year": 2022, "venue": "RSC Advances", "doi": "10.1039/d2ra03303h", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1039/d2ra03303h", "pdf_url": "https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlepdf/2022/ra/d2ra03303h", "abstract": "(LLZO) garnet material has led to poor reproducibility with a wide range of ionic conductivities reported in the literature. In this study, the role of precursor homogeneity in controlling the compositional and structural evolution of Al-doped LLZO is explored. A novel solution-based synthesis approach is employed to demonstrate enhanced atomic-scale mixing of the starting materials in comparison to conventional solid-state preparation methods. Through this technique, it is shown that the stability and formation temperature of the highly conductive cubic phase is directly impacted by the spatial distribution of the doping element and reactant species in the precursor mixture. Precursor homogeneity was also an important factor in mitigating the formation of unwanted secondary impurities. These findings can be used to guide the synthesis of SSEs with reproducible material characteristics and enhanced electrolytic performance.", "citation_count": 37, "_query": "LLZO cover powder mother powder lithium volatilization", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    28	{"id": "862c01178a6dcc534aa6410b59ae563a233246ac", "title": "Searching for low-cost Li MO compounds for compensating Li-loss in sintering of Li-Garnet solid electrolyte", "authors": ["Xiao Huang", "Zhen Song", "Tongping Xiu", "Badding Michael Edward", "Z. Wen"], "year": 2019, "venue": "Journal of Materiomics", "doi": "10.1016/j.jmat.2018.09.004", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/862c01178a6dcc534aa6410b59ae563a233246ac", "pdf_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352847818300765/pdf", "abstract": "Abstract Cubic Li-Garnet Li7La3Zr2O12 (c-LLZO) is one of the promising solid-state electrolyte candidates for the next generation high safety solid-state batteries. However, preparing the electrolyte has many challenges. Li-loss during high temperature sintering is one of them. Mother powder with the same component of green garnet pellets is often used to co-fire with the pellets to compensate the Li-loss. Due to the high weight ratio of rare earth element La and non-recyclability of mother powder, it is worthy to explore low-cost mother powders to replace them. In this paper, low cost compounds such as Li5AlO4, Li2TiO3, Li2SiO3, Li4SiO4 and (Li2O)x-(ZrO2)1-x (x = 0.6–0.8) are investigated for substitution of the mother powder for compensating Li-loss during the sintering of LLZO. Dense Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO) samples have been prepared by sintering with (Li2O)0.733(ZrO2)0.267 powder at 1150 °C for 5 h with the relative density of 95% and conductivity of 5.7 × 10−4 S cm−1 at 25 °C, which show same performance with LLZTO ceramics sintered with LLZO mother powder.", "citation_count": 24, "_query": "LLZO LLZTO sintering aid Li3BO3 low temperature sintering", "sources": ["semanticscholar"]}
    29	{"id": "e6e03ae29341644ad6abba750536a84178ba25aa", "title": "Li5AlO4-Assisted Low-Temperature Sintering of Dense Li7La3Zr2O12 Solid Electrolyte with High Critical Current Density.", "authors": ["Hao Zhou", "Yongjiang Zhou", "Xiaoyi Li", "Xiao Huang", "Bingbing Tian"], "year": 2024, "venue": "ACS Applied Materials and Interfaces", "doi": "10.1021/acsami.3c17606", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e6e03ae29341644ad6abba750536a84178ba25aa", "pdf_url": "", "abstract": "In recent years, solid electrolytes (SEs) have been developed a lot due to the superior safety of solid-state batteries (SSBs) upon liquid electrolyte-based commercial batteries. Among them, garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is one of the few SEs that is stable to lithium anode with high Li+ conductivity and the feasibility of preparation under ambient air, which makes it a promising candidate for fabricating SSBs. However, high sintering temperature (>1200 °C) prevents its large-scale production, further hindering its application. In this work, the Li5AlO4 sintering aid is proposed to decrease the sintering temperature and modify the grain boundaries of LLZO ceramics. Li5AlO4 generates in situ Li2O atmosphere and molten Li-Al-O compounds at relatively low temperatures to facilitate the gas-liquid-solid material transportation among raw LLZO grains, which decreases the densification temperature over 150 °C and strengthens the grain boundaries against lithium dendrites. As an example, Ta-doped LLZO ceramics without excessive Li sintered with 2 wt % Li5AlO4 at 1050 °C delivered high relative density > 94%, an ionic conductivity of 6.7 × 10-4 S cm-1, and an excellent critical current density (CCD) of 1.5 mA cm-2 at room temperature. In comparison, Ta-doped LLZO with 15% excessive Li sintered at 1200 °C delivered low relative density < 89%, a low ionic conductivity of ∼2 × 10-4 S cm-1, and a poor CCD of 0.5 mA cm-2. Li symmetric cells and Li-LFP full cells fabricated with Li5AlO4-assised ceramics were stably cycled at 0.2 mA cm-2 over 2000 h and at 0.8C over 100 cycles, respectively.", "citation_count": 16, "_query": "LLZO LLZTO sintering aid Li3BO3 low temperature sintering", "sources": ["semanticscholar"]}
    30	{"id": "10.20964/2019.10.22", "title": "Investigation of Al2O3 Crucible Contamination induced by extra Li2CO3 during Li7La3Zr2O12 Solid Electrolyte Sintering process", "authors": ["Weijie Lan", "Dongliang Lu", "Ruirui Zhao", "Hongyu Chen"], "year": 2019, "venue": "International Journal of Electrochemical Science", "doi": "10.20964/2019.10.22", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.20964/2019.10.22", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 15, "_query": "LLZO crucible aluminum contamination sintering", "sources": ["crossref"]}
    31	{"id": "W4377011899", "title": "Uniform Densification of Garnet Electrolyte for Solid‐State Lithium Batteries", "authors": ["Zhihao Guo", "Qihou Li", "Xinhai Li", "Zhixing Wang", "Huajun Guo", "Wenjie Peng", "Guangchao Li", "Guochun Yan", "Jiexi Wang"], "year": 2023, "venue": "Small Methods", "doi": "10.1002/smtd.202300232", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/smtd.202300232", "pdf_url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/smtd.202300232", "abstract": "Abstract Highly uniformly dense garnet type solid‐state electrolyte plays a significant role in determining the performance of solid‐state lithium batteries. Herein, a rational powder‐covering sintering strategy is proposed and demonstrated, in which narrow‐particle‐size‐distribution fine powder and uniform sintering temperature distribution are considered as very significant factors. It is suggested that powder materials with wider particle size distribution dramatically decrease the densified level of electrolytes. Slow temperature elevating rate and the overhead structure of bearing table are found to be beneficial to uniform densification. Moreover, the uniform densification process of sintering solid‐state electrolyte is studied both microscopically and macroscopically, which can be divided into three phases according to the grain growing evolution and linear shrinkage patterns. The ionic conductivity of the as‐prepared Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO) garnet electrolyte is determined to be 0.73 mS cm−1 at 303 K with an activation energy of 0.37 eV. The Li/LLZTO/Li symmetric cell exhibits a small interfacial impedance of 8.49 Ω cm2 and a high apparent critical current density of 2.15 mA cm−2 and also can be cycled for 1000 h continuously without short‐circuit. Such results indicate the good feasibility of as‐proposed sintering strategy to prepare uniformly dense garnet type solid‐state electrolytes for solid‐state lithium batteries.", "citation_count": 13, "_query": "LLZTO densification grain boundary total conductivity", "sources": ["openalex"]}
    32	{"id": "W2996301349", "title": "Submicron-Sized Nb-Doped Lithium Garnet for High Ionic Conductivity Solid Electrolyte and Performance of Quasi-Solid-State Lithium Battery", "authors": ["Ji Yan", "Cankai Zhou", "Feng Lin", "Bingjing Li", "Feifan Yang", "Huali Zhu", "Junfei Duan", "Zhaoyong Chen"], "year": 2020, "venue": "Materials", "doi": "10.3390/ma13030560", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/ma13030560", "pdf_url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1944/13/3/560/pdf", "abstract": "The garnet Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has been widely investigated because of its high conductivity, wide electrochemical window, and chemical stability with regards to lithium metal. However, the usual preparation process of LLZO requires high-temperature sintering for a long time and a lot of mother powder to compensate for lithium evaporation. In this study submicron Li6.6La3Zr1.6Nb0.4O12 (LLZNO) powder―which has a stable cubic phase and high sintering activity―was prepared using the conventional solid-state reaction and the attrition milling process, and Li stoichiometric LLZNO ceramics were obtained by sintering this powder―which is difficult to control under high sintering temperatures and when sintered for a long time―at a relatively low temperature or for a short amount of time. The particle-size distribution, phase structure, microstructure, distribution of elements, total ionic conductivity, relative density, and activation energy of the submicron LLZNO powder and the LLZNO ceramics were tested and analyzed using laser diffraction particle-size analyzer (LD), X-Ray Diffraction (XRD), Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM), Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS), and the Archimedean method. The total ionic conductivity of samples sintered at 1200 °C for 30 min was 5.09 × 10−4 S·cm−1, the activation energy was 0.311 eV, and the relative density was 87.3%. When the samples were sintered at 1150 °C for 60 min the total ionic conductivity was 3.49 × 10−4 S·cm−1, the activation energy was 0.316 eV, and the relative density was 90.4%. At the same time, quasi-solid-state batteries were assembled with LiMn2O4 as the positive electrode and submicron LLZNO powder as the solid-state electrolyte. After 50 cycles, the discharge specific capacity was 105.5 mAh/g and the columbic efficiency was above 95%.", "citation_count": 64, "sources": ["openalex"]}
    33	{"id": "10.1016/j.materresbull.2014.01.041", "title": "Lithium stoichiometry of solid electrolytes based on tetragonal Li7La3Zr2O12", "authors": ["E.A. Il’ina", "A.A. Raskovalov", "P.Y. Shevelin", "V.I. Voronin", "I.F. Berger", "N.A. Zhyravlev"], "year": 2014, "venue": "Materials Research Bulletin", "doi": "10.1016/j.materresbull.2014.01.041", "arxiv_id": null, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.materresbull.2014.01.041", "pdf_url": null, "abstract": null, "citation_count": 13, "sources": ["crossref"]}
